# Debian Live runtime. These are what turn a plain chroot into a live system.

live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
systemd-sysv

# --- Live user creation (DO NOT DROP) ---
# live-config's 0030-user-setup component is a no-op unless the `user-setup`
# PACKAGE is installed — it literally starts with:
#     if ! pkg_is_installed "user-setup" ...; then exit 0; fi
# Without it the `irn` user named by `username=irn` in --bootappend-live is
# never created, so tty1's autologin getty and greetd BOTH die at boot with
# "Authentication failure" / "could not authenticate" — a black console instead
# of a desktop. Verified by boot-testing an image built without it
# (./boot-test.sh). The user is created at BOOT, not at build time, on purpose:
# that keeps it out of the squashfs, so Calamares-installed systems get only the
# account the operator creates in the installer.
user-setup

# `sudo` is required by two things: live-config's 0040-sudo component (grants
# the live user passwordless sudo, without which nothing in the live session can
# gain root) and Debian's own Calamares wrapper /usr/bin/calamares-install-debian,
# which shells out to `sudo` twice and then `pkexec calamares`. No sudo, no
# installer — and the LUKS-at-install story dies with it.
sudo
